Creating a script to deny access to a website or service can be done in various ways depending on the context and the technology stack you are using. Below, I'll provide a simple example using a basic HTML page and a server-side script in Node.js. This example will demonstrate how to deny access based on a specific condition (e.g., a user not being logged in).

### HTML Page (index.html)
This is a simple HTML page that will be shown to users who are denied access.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Access Denied</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 50px;
        }
        h1 {
            color: #ff0000;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Access Denied</h1>
    <p>You do not have permission to access this page.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

### Node.js Server (server.js)
This Node.js script sets up a basic server that checks if a user is logged in. If not, it serves the "Access Denied" page.

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');

// Simulate a user session
const userSession = {
    isLoggedIn: false // Change this to true to allow access
};

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    if (req.url === '/' && !userSession.isLoggedIn) {
        // User is not logged in, serve the access denied page
        fs.readFile(path.join(__dirname, 'index.html'), (err, data) => {
            if (err) {
                res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
                res.end('Internal Server Error');
            } else {
                res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
                res.end(data);
            }
        });
    } else {
        // User is logged in, serve the main page or any other page
        res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        res.end('Welcome to the website!');
    }
});

const PORT = 3000;
server.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server is running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

### How It Works
1. **HTML Page**: The `index.html` file contains a simple message indicating that access is denied.
2. **Node.js Server**: The `server.js` file sets up an HTTP server that listens on port 3000. It checks if the user is logged in by looking at the `userSession.isLoggedIn` property.
   - If the user is not logged in (`userSession.isLoggedIn` is `false`), it serves the `index.html` file.
   - If the user is logged in (`userSession.isLoggedIn` is `true`), it serves a welcome message.

